    <hr>

    <h3>2. The Persistence Model (Relational Schema &amp; Curriculum Repository)</h3>
    <p>
      The persistence model organizes the ecosystem across five architectural clusters:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Curriculum &amp; Narrative:</b> <code>apps</code> defines client targets, while <code>curriculum_nav_items</code> provides a recursive hierarchical topic tree linking to <code>segments</code> (the modular chapters) and their prerequisite progression graph (<code>segment_prerequisites</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Axiomatic Verification Engine:</b> <code>formal_statements</code> models mathematical theorems and definitions with self-referential parent-child hierarchies, certified directly by the Lean kernel and indexed in <code>lean_verifications</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>First-Class Interactive Demos:</b> <code>interactive_demos</code> registers independent visual exploration sandboxes (Binary Tree Demos, Binary Interval Demos, and Truth Table Demos) that connect to both formal statements and chapter references.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Active Computational Stencils:</b> <code>directed_equations</code> defines directional computational stencils, grounded by <code>mode_slots</code> (parameter ranges, symbols, units) and <code>verified_presets</code> (concrete JSON test cases). The relational bridge <code>equation_statement_links</code> explicitly connects these active formulas to the governing formal statements they instantiate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Centralized Citation Hub:</b> <code>segment_references</code> links inline <code>&lt;fs-ref&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;fsd-ref&gt;</code>, calculator, and demo citations in HTML segments directly to their governing formal statements, directed equations, verified presets, and interactive visual applications.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h3>3. The Verification &amp; Utilities Infrastructure Model (Lean 4 &amp; Compiler Pipeline)</h3>
    <p>
      Lean 4 is an open-source programming language and interactive theorem prover based on the Calculus of Inductive Constructions. Lean supports formal statements that can be verified and used for calculation. MWM uses Lean as an authoritative formal verifier to certify structural consistency and commutative algebraic laws, while handling concrete numeric calculation—and its relation to formal statements—internally via the dyadic arithmetic engine.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Middle Way Mathematics public website (<code>middlewaymath.app</code>) is deployed as a static web application without a live runtime dependency on Lean. Instead, a dedicated suite of Node utilities and build-time pipelines compiles, verifies, and caches proof information from the local Lean installation into production assets. Additionally, during local authoring, an interactive compiler bridge provides live real-time feedback against the active Lean kernel, as illustrated in the following architecture diagram:
    </p>

    <div align="center" style="margin: 16px 0; overflow-x: auto;">
