  (55, 'lean4GenEdProposal', 54, 'The Public Utility Model for Formal Science', 'lean4-gen-ed-proposal', '
<div class="card">
      <h1>The Public Utility Model for Formal Science</h1>
      <h2>— Prototyping an Open Civic Infrastructure for Formal Verification, Machine-Checked Proof, and Autonomous Curricula —</h2>

      <!-- Core Proposal Statement Up Front -->
      <div class="box-blue" style="border-left: 5px solid #2563eb; padding: 18px 20px;">
        <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; color: #1d4ed8; font-size: 18px; border: none; padding: 0;">
          Core Proposal: Prototyping the Educational Resource Hub (ERH)
        </h3>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;">
          We propose the prototyping and deployment of a publicly available, community-supported <b>Educational Resource Hub (ERH)</b> for foundational formal science and mathematical verification services. 
          By treating formal proof verification as shared civic infrastructure rather than a proprietary commodity, the Hub fulfills a fundamental <b>dual role</b>:
        </p>
        <ol style="margin: 0; padding-left: 22px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;">
          <li><b>Generic Public-Domain Verification APIs:</b> Hosting high-performance, stateless microservice endpoints for formal theorem proving and type verification (<b>Lean 4</b>)—freely queryable by any educational client application across the internet.</li>
          <li><b>Federated Curricular Storage via a Single DBMS:</b> Providing managed database and storage resources within a single, centralized DBMS (such as PostgreSQL), where recognized <b>"Schools of Thought"</b> are allocated dedicated database schemas to curate and maintain their respective <b>Pedagogic Databases (PDBs)</b> without the burden of database server administration.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h3>1. The Problem: The DevOps Friction Wall &amp; The Conflation Trap</h3>
      <p>
        The open-source formal science community has produced astonishing tools that represent the summit of mathematical rigor:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Lean 4:</b> An interactive theorem prover and programming language offering absolute, machine-checked certainty for formal mathematical claims and verified computational definitions.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Yet formal theorem provers remain almost entirely absent from general education classrooms. The barrier is not that students cannot grasp formal logic and constructive reasoning; the barrier is <b>operational friction</b> caused by the conflation of <i>formal proof execution</i> with <i>curriculum delivery</i>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Local Install Pain:</b> Installing Lean 4, Git, Elan, and specialized VS Code extensions on diverse student laptops (or locked-down school Chromebooks) is an insurmountable logistical burden for educators.</li>
        <li><b>The Personal Cloud Bill:</b> To offer live interactive proof checking via the web, an independent curriculum creator or department must manage container clusters, configure reverse proxies, monitor security, and pay monthly cloud hosting bills out of their own pocket.</li>
        <li><b>The Commercial Trap:</b> In the absence of open civic infrastructure, educational institutions fall prey to proprietary SaaS platforms that lock learning behind expensive per-seat licensing fees and opaque software stacks.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="box-amber">
        <b>The Blue-Collar Reality:</b><br>
        A carpentry teacher isn''t asked to smelt their own steel for hand saws, and a chemistry teacher isn''t asked to build a municipal water treatment plant to get clean tap water for lab beakers. Why do we expect formal science teachers and curriculum authors to run their own cloud server clusters just to give students access to a verified theorem checker?
      </div>

      <h3>2. The Solution: Decoupling Generic Verification from Curricular Content</h3>
      <p>
        The most cost-effective and democratic intervention open-science foundations (such as the NSF, Sloan, or university consortia) can make is <b>patronage of shared public educational service hubs</b>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Centrally Hosted, Publicly Available:</b> Secure, containerized server clusters running stateless Lean 4 verification workers, managed by open-source infrastructure administrators.</li>
        <li><b>Simple, Open Microservice APIs:</b> Standard HTTP/JSON and WebSocket endpoints where any client application can send a formal statement or proof snippet and receive a structured certificate of validity (or proof state diagnostics).</li>
        <li><b>Universal Client Access:</b> Any independent educational project, hobbyist web app, undergraduate seminar, or student portal can freely query the hub as a public utility—just like querying an open NTP time server or public DNS.</li>
        <li><b>Curricular Decoupling:</b> The Hub itself has zero editorial bias or curricular gatekeeping. It provides formal verification horsepower and database housing, while independent academic communities curate their own pedagogic structures.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Federated Multi-Role Architecture: 4 Distinct Roles Across 2 Operational Planes</h3>
      <p>
        The <i>Middle Way Mathematics</i> architecture resolves the historic bottleneck of educational software by formalizing a clean separation across two operational planes—<b>generic compute utilities</b> versus <b>state-bearing pedagogic databases (PDBs)</b> hosted as dedicated schemas in a single DBMS—distributed across four clearly delimited roles:
      </p>

      <!-- Federated Architecture Vector Diagram -->
      <div style="margin: 28px 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); overflow-x: auto;">
        <h4 style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 1.05rem; color: #0f172a; text-align: center; font-weight: 700;">
          Federated Architecture: Public Utility Compute, Curricular Schemas &amp; Autonomous Delivery
        </h4>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 920 600" width="100%" height="auto" style="max-width: 920px; display: block; margin: 0 auto; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, ''Segoe UI'', Roboto, sans-serif;">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="6" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 1 L 10 5 L 0 9 z" fill="#1e293b" />
            </marker>
            <marker id="arrow-blue" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="6" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 1 L 10 5 L 0 9 z" fill="#2563eb" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <!-- Background Canvas Boundary -->
          <rect x="10" y="10" width="900" height="580" rx="14" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1.5" />

          <!-- 1. Top Section: Hub Administrator -->
          <g transform="translate(40, 25)">
            <!-- Stick Figure: Hub Admin -->
            <circle cx="35" cy="22" r="14" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <line x1="35" y1="36" x2="35" y2="72" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <line x1="15" y1="50" x2="55" y2="50" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <line x1="35" y1="72" x2="18" y2="105" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <line x1="35" y1="72" x2="52" y2="105" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <text x="35" y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="13" fill="#0f172a">Hub Admin</text>

            <!-- Capabilities Card -->
            <rect x="90" y="5" width="220" height="115" rx="6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <rect x="90" y="5" width="220" height="28" rx="6" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <rect x="98" y="13" width="10" height="10" fill="#cbd5e1" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1" />
            <line x1="100" y1="18" x2="106" y2="18" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="1.5" />
